<div class="qandaset block" id="av_1.0-ch17_s01_s03_qa01_ans">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Book:_General_Chemistry:_Principles_Patterns_and_Applications_(Averill)/17:_Solubility_and_Complexation_Equilibria/17.02:_Determining_the_Solubility_of_Ionic_Compounds" class="mt-section"><span id="Answers"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-349761 editable">Answers</h3>

<ol class="qandadiv">
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349761" id="av_1.0-ch17_s01_s03_qa01_qd01_q01_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349761"><em>K</em><sub>sp</sub> = [Ag<sup>+</sup>][I<sup>&minus;</sup>]</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349761"><em>K</em><sub>sp</sub> = [Ca<sup>2+</sup>][F<sup>&minus;</sup>]<sup>2</sup></li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349761"><em>K</em><sub>sp</sub> = [Pb<sup>2+</sup>][Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>]<sup>2</sup></li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349761"><em>K</em><sub>sp</sub> = [Ag<sup>+</sup>]<sup>2</sup>[CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>]</li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349761" id="av_1.0-ch17_s01_s03_qa01_qd01_q02_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349761" id="av_1.0-ch17_s01_s03_qa01_qd01_q03_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349761" id="av_1.0-ch17_s01_s03_qa01_qd01_q04_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349761" id="av_1.0-ch17_s01_s03_qa01_qd01_q05_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349761">For a 1:1 salt, the molar solubility is simply \(\sqrt{K_{\textrm{sp}}}\) ; for a 2:1 salt, the molar solubility is \(\sqrt[3]{K_{\textrm{sp}}/4}\)<span class="inlineequation">&gt;</span> Consequently, the magnitudes of <em>K</em><sub>sp</sub> can be correlated with molar solubility <em>only</em> if the salts have the same stoichiometry.</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349761" id="av_1.0-ch17_s01_s03_qa01_qd01_q06_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349761" id="av_1.0-ch17_s01_s03_qa01_qd01_q07_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349761">Because of the common ion effect. Adding a soluble Mg<sup>2+</sup> salt increases [Mg<sup>2+</sup>] in solution, and Le Chatelier&rsquo;s principle predicts that this will shift the solubility equilibrium of MgCO<sub>3</sub> to the left, decreasing its solubility.</p>
    </div>
    </li>
</ol>
</div></div>

